WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.830
When the #MeToo movement first took off

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:06.160
and we were getting women coming forward

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.590
and telling their stories
of harassment, assault

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:12.660
or discrimination in their workplace.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.100
We're like this is only a matter of time

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.400
before we get some really good stories

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:16.960
coming out of the
financial industry because

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.543
we know that it exists.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.060
Wall Street just like lots of areas has

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.670
some serious and pretty deep problems.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:29.670
But a big cultural
moment has not happened.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:32.470
Now to the #MeToo movement
growing this morning

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:34.910
in the wake of the
Harvey Weinstein scandal.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:37.790
High powered men across
entertainment, media,

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.480
technology and politics
have been brought down

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.900
by women who feel empowered
to tell their stories.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.110
But on Wall Street a system of silence

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.810
has protected the status quo.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:50.610
The primary difference
for women on Wall Street

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:53.850
versus other industries is money.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:58.850
And money is power and Wall
Street has the most money

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:02.930
and most power of any industry.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.980
You know story by story we get this peek

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:09.440
into this machine, different
arms of the machine.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.970
Those arms explain why
there was no revolution

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:13.380
in finance this year.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.970
Instead there were rare
moments of revelation

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:17.810
that pierced the silence.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.020
Keeping people quiet
doesn't make it better

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:21.320
for the business.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:25.210
You don't hire the best
people if you exclude people

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:26.820
who are brave.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.460
This is the story of the forces

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:29.990
that have kept the #MeToo reckoning

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:31.470
from hitting Wall Street.

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:32.703
At least so far.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.540
Women didn't want to come
forward with their stories.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.370
They didn't want to go on the record.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.900
The women I talked to
didn't even want to name

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.800
the people who had assaulted
them or hurt their careers

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.130
in a particular way.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:47.860
And we kind of had to take a step back

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.100
and say why is that happening.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.680
And evaluate what is the
culture in Wall Street

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.090
or the system in Wall Street

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.690
that's keeping women from doing that?

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:59.020
There is a machine of silence

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.710
that we've learned about that has to do

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:03.420
with arbitration and human resources

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.650
and public relations and a culture of fear

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:08.260
and a culture of money.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.490
And all those things together combine

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:13.600
to create kind of a
wall between allegations

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:14.900
and transparency.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.400
First of all you have this
very male dominated industry.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.610
About 26% of senior
executives on Wall Street

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:24.600
are women and when you
get to the CEO level

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.330
it's abysmally low.

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:28.870
Women only make up 8% of CEOs

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:31.590
at major financial institutions.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.820
And in the history of Wall
Street, no woman has ever led

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:38.110
any of the six largest U.S. banks.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:40.580
I remember Wall Street women saying to me,

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.750
what do you think Wall Street values?

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:46.950
They value relationships
and they value secrecy

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:50.850
and they value loyalty and if I dare

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.760
to go against those
things, I will be exiled.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.020
And if someone does decide to complain

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:57.750
it isn't done in public.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.300
But rather hidden behind
a widespread system

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:01.660
of arbitration.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.300
Arbitration needs to be relabeled.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.680
It needs to be called forced silence.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:08.510
Arbitration is a private system

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.730
that takes fights behind closed doors

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.720
away from a judge and jury.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.440
It was originally used to
solve industry disputes

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.880
but Wall Street has helped
it spread to all kinds

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.930
of fights including harassment.

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:23.700
Civil Rights issues should
be dealt with in court.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.720
Civil Rights issues should be transparent.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.010
That's Lee Stowell, a Wall Street veteran

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:31.380
whose suing Cantor
Fitzgerald for harassment,

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.820
discrimination and retaliation.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:35.850
But that's just a part of her fight.

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:39.220
She's also battling to
stay away from arbitration.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.210
I want to go to court because
I want it to be transparent.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:43.780
It's not a female thing.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.570
It's not a male thing, it's a human thing.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:48.860
What's so wild for us as
reporters following Lee Stowell's

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:53.150
story is that she had
not one but apparently

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:55.730
two arbitration agreements Cantor says.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:58.650
So basically she said,
I'm not supposed to be

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:01.240
thrown out of court, I'm
supposed to stay in this venue

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:02.690
and the court agreed with her.

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:04.510
Cantor denies her allegations

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.660
and is appealing the decision
to let her stay in court.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.760
But these days judges all the
way up to the Supreme Court

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:13.270
have been siding with arbitration.

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:15.360
From Wall Street's perspective that system

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.390
is quicker, cheaper,
quieter and just as fair

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:19.400
as going to court.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.740
Women like Lee see it differently.

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:24.250
For those of us who are not corporations,

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.930
we want to be able to show the evidence

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:31.680
and we want to be able to change behavior.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.930
Women are not learning about each other.

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:37.790
They're being isolated from each other.

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:40.310
So they can't really come together

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:44.920
and bring something bigger
against a bank or an institution

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.300
and say, you have a major problem here

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:49.040
because it's all in the hush-hush system

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:50.800
that nobody knows about.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:53.040
While Lee was fighting
against arbitration,

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.410
women thousands of miles
away were struggling

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:58.420
to have their stories heard
inside Lloyd's of London.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:02.100
Lloyd's is a 331-year-old
exchange for the worldwide

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.393
insurance market that's
steeped in tradition.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:07.850
So this is the large
loss book, where we show

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:11.100
a book for the current day
and a corresponding book

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.500
from 100 years ago.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:16.710
Much of the work there is
still done face-to-face

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.560
by mostly middle-aged white men on paper

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.250
and sealed with rubber stamps.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.280
But reporting from Bloomberg uncovered

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.000
that Lloyd's also has
a deep seated tradition

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:29.210
of sexual harassment.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:32.780
We spoke to women who had
been attacked by their bosses

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:35.010
in pubs and taxis.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:37.800
And we'd spoken to other
women who were deluged

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.260
with text, graphic text, graphic emails

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:44.710
from their employers
basically soliciting for sex.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.800
What's so sinister about these stories

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.610
isn't just the picture they
paint of rich men behaving

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:52.780
badly but the way HR protected them.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:56.491
At least in my experience
for the Wall Street firms.

00:05:56.491 --> 00:06:00.530
Most of the time say that
they simply can't go to HR.

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:02.446
It's just not an option.

00:06:02.446 --> 00:06:07.200
Because if they do, HR is
then going to report them

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.940
to either the exact male that
they just complained about

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:16.940
or somebody else in a position
to make things difficult.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:19.120
But of the ones who did go to HR,

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:22.940
they reported being
discouraged from pursuing

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:25.050
their complaints further.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.640
They were warned about the
possibility of being marked out

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.770
as difficult women.

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:31.850
So they kept quiet.

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:34.490
Lloyd's first female CEO, Inga Beale,

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:36.575
tried to change that.

00:06:36.575 --> 00:06:39.180
But she was met with hostility,
including a death threat.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.500
And was eventually replaced
by John Neal in 2018.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:45.060
Bloomberg's reporting
hit a few months later.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:47.570
Two executives at the
company have now resigned

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:50.420
following allegations
of sexual harassment.

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:52.459
Sometimes when we're reporting
our stories like this

00:06:52.459 --> 00:06:55.470
companies just deny what we find

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:57.390
and say, look everything is fine.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:59.970
In the case of Lloyd's, Gavin's
reporting was so powerful

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:02.260
that the CEO of this insurance market

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:04.650
came out afterwards and said,
yeah, this is pretty much

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:07.570
unacceptable and we will change.

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:11.060
This is not the Lloyd's
that I want to be part of

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:14.306
and not the Lloyd's that
many of my colleagues feel

00:07:14.306 --> 00:07:16.080
they want to be part of either.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.420
Lloyd's unveiled a whistleblower hotline,

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:21.240
banned alcohol and
announced a lifetime ban

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:22.810
for sexual harassers.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:27.660
Whether there is any long-term
impact from these measures

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:29.953
it's too early, I think to tell.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.430
And while the Lloyd's story

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:35.220
showed how women's stories
can help bring an antiquated

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:37.770
institution into the 21st Century,

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:40.060
a conference in California
showed what happens

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:43.260
when the industry's bystanders
break their silence.

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:44.640
Everyone it's Alex Chalekian,

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.579
just got back from the
Tiburone CEO Summit dinner.

00:07:47.579 --> 00:07:50.690
In October of 2019, Alex Chalekian

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:53.440
who runs a firm called
Lake Avenue Financial

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:55.780
was attending a conference
and did something unheard

00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:56.900
of on Wall Street.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:58.730
He publicly spoke out against someone

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:01.300
at an invite-only conference.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:03.010
Things that were said by Ken Fisher

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:05.130
were just absolutely horrifying.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:07.550
He made comments about genitalia.

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:11.363
He talked about picking up on a girl

00:08:11.363 --> 00:08:14.030
and don't show them what's in your pants.

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:16.170
And shortly after, Bloomberg reporter

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:18.810
Sabrina Willmer was able
to speak to Ken Fisher

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:19.690
on the phone.

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:22.630
His initial reaction
is that he didn't think

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:25.050
it was a big deal because he's been saying

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:27.450
this a very long time.

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:28.930
And nobody has questioned it.

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:31.090
Ken Fisher was speaking
in a way that he was used

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:35.470
to speaking which was
gross, quite frankly.

00:08:35.470 --> 00:08:37.406
I don't know if I could
but if was 30-years-old

00:08:37.406 --> 00:08:40.063
and I had to do over
again, I'd have more sex.

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:43.930
While I could, while I could.

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:47.946
Once you get older you're
like a Christmas tree,

00:08:47.946 --> 00:08:50.833
you're firm once a year and
the balls are for decoration.

00:08:51.897 --> 00:08:53.440
But instead of awkward laugher

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:56.560
behind closed doors, this
year Fisher's institutional

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:58.920
clients from Texas to New Hampshire yanked

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:02.250
about $4 billion from his investment firm.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:05.220
Fisher CEO, Damian
Ornani, even went directly

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:08.699
in front of a Los Angeles
pension board to apologize.

00:09:08.699 --> 00:09:10.982
I'd just like to apologize.

00:09:10.982 --> 00:09:14.632
That we're here today talking about this

00:09:14.632 --> 00:09:17.602
and these inappropriate comments happened

00:09:17.602 --> 00:09:18.782
in the first place.

00:09:18.782 --> 00:09:22.833
Ken knows they were wrong,
I know they are wrong.

00:09:22.833 --> 00:09:24.020
But for pension board members

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:27.550
like Brian Pendleton that
apology wasn't enough.

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:29.270
I'm incredibly proud
of what the L.A. Police

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:31.770
and Fire Pension Commission
did by decisively

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.960
terminating the contract with Ken Fisher.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.730
That it hopefully sends a loud message

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:38.320
to the financial community.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:41.500
While about $4 billion
was lost, the firm still

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:43.433
manages $115 billion.

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:46.500
And the company has said
that since the incident

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:49.150
their assets have actually grown.

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:51.490
And we talked to each
other inside the newsroom

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:56.100
and we ask is this as much
change as we're gonna see.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:10:00.320
It's plausible that is as
far from the status quo

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:03.030
as things are gonna veer
and things will get back

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:04.470
to business as usual.

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:07.780
I'm not in favor of outing other women

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:09.940
but I will say that women
have reached out to me

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:13.545
and told me that we
need to keep this going.

00:10:13.545 --> 00:10:17.024
I'm certainly a drop
in bucket to the issue

00:10:17.024 --> 00:10:19.620
and the only way to change
that is for a couple

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:21.410
of people to stand up and I'm hopeful

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:23.760
that other people will stand next to me.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.510
We're never going to stop
trying to tell women's stories.

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:29.480
What we can hope for is
that the industry is moving

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:32.520
in a direction in which
more women feel comfortable

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:33.853
telling their stories.

